# Portable publication images

A portable book carries its illustrations alongside its text. Pre-rendered PNG,
JPEG and PDF images need neither a browser nor a conversion utility: the exporter
copies their bytes and lets LaTeX fit them to the page. This boundary separates
asset transport from diagram rendering. Ordinary Mermaid fences use an optional
explicit system-browser adapter; an unavailable renderer is an error, never a
reason to print diagram source as code or silently omit the figure.

A standalone Markdown image is resolved relative to its source chapter. Titles
become plain captions, falling back to plain alt text. Inline images, formatted
alt text, raw SVG, remote URLs, queries and fragments are rejected. PDF inclusion
uses page one; authors must supply a single-page figure when that distinction
matters. Binary signatures are checked, not full file validity: these are trusted
publication inputs, not a PDF/image malware sanitizer.


## Narrow filesystem contract

All filesystem access is injected. The CLI checks every component for symlinks,
requires regular files and bounds reads before allocating buffers. The planner
also bounds returned bytes, so test adapters cannot accidentally bypass its limit.
It prepares bytes only; the existing output transaction owns publication.

```ts : <<publication asset imports>>=
import { createHash } from 'crypto';
import { posix } from 'path';
import { safeRelativePath, ParsedProject } from './engine';
import { preparePandoc, PublicationAsset } from './publication-pandoc';
import type { PublicationLinkResolver } from './publication-pandoc';
import type { FontInputIO } from './publication-fonts';
import type { DiagramBytes } from './publication-mermaid';
export const maximumAssetBytes = 32 * 1024 * 1024;
export interface AssetPlan {
    inputs: string[];
    files: { path: string; content: Uint8Array }[];
    paths: Map<string, string>;
}
export const assetKey = (asset: PublicationAsset): string => JSON.stringify([asset.kind, asset.at.path, asset.source]);
const hash = (data: string | Uint8Array): string => createHash('sha256').update(data).digest('hex');
```

Decode URI escaping exactly once, reject URL syntax before and after decoding,
then normalize relative to the chapter directory. Parent steps are allowed only
when the resulting portable path stays within the project root. No absolute path,
backslash or platform-dependent drive syntax reaches the filesystem adapter.

```ts : <<publication asset path>>=
export function assetInput(asset: PublicationAsset): string {
    let source: string;
    try { source = decodeURIComponent(asset.source); }
    catch { throw new Error('Image path has invalid URI escaping'); }
    const invalid = (value: string): boolean => !value || /^[\/]/.test(value) || /[\\:?#\u0000-\u001f\u007f]/.test(value);
    if (invalid(asset.source) || invalid(source)) {
        throw new Error('Image requires a local relative path without queries or fragments');
    }
    const result = safeRelativePath(posix.join(posix.dirname(asset.at.path), source));
    if (!result) { throw new Error('Image path escapes the project or is not portable'); }
    return result;
}
```

Extensions must match signatures. JPEG spellings share one canonical suffix;
identical content deduplicates even when source names differ. Full SHA-256 names
contain no author-controlled TeX syntax and remain stable on relocation.

```ts : <<publication asset format>>=
function assetFormat(input: string, bytes: Uint8Array): string {
    if (!bytes.length || bytes.length > maximumAssetBytes) { throw new Error('Image must be 1–33554432 bytes'); }
    const data = Buffer.from(bytes);
    const ext = posix.extname(input).toLowerCase();
    if (ext === '.png' && data.subarray(0, 8).equals(Buffer.from('89504e470d0a1a0a', 'hex'))) { return 'png'; }
    if (/^\.jpe?g$/.test(ext) && data[0] === 255 && data[1] === 216 && data[2] === 255) { return 'jpg'; }
    if (ext === '.pdf' && data.subarray(0, 5).toString('ascii') === '%PDF-') { return 'pdf'; }
    throw new Error('Image must be PNG, JPEG or PDF with a matching signature; SVG is unsupported');
}
```

The total plan is bounded at 128 MiB of unique content and 1024 requests. All
aliases remain protected inputs even when their bytes deduplicate. Failures name
the source chapter and line; no partially planned output is published.

```ts : <<publication asset plan>>=
export async function planAssets(assets: readonly PublicationAsset[], output: string, io: FontInputIO,
    render?: (assets: PublicationAsset[]) => Promise<Map<string, DiagramBytes>>): Promise<AssetPlan> {
    if (assets.length > 1024) { throw new Error('Publication exceeds 1024 image requests'); }
    const directory = `lit-assets-${hash(posix.basename(output))}`;
    const plan: AssetPlan = { inputs: [], files: [], paths: new Map() };
    const inputs = new Map<string, string>(), files = new Set<string>();
    let total = 0;
    <<publication asset addition>>
    for (const asset of assets.filter(asset => asset.kind === 'image')) {
        try {
            <<publication asset prepare one>>
        } catch (error) { throw new Error(`${asset.at.path}:${asset.at.line + 1}: ${String(error)}`); }
    }
    plan.inputs = [...inputs.keys()];
    <<publication diagram companions>>
    return plan;
}
```

Each source file is read once. A new filename with the same content gets the same
companion; each occurrence retains its own caption and container width.

```ts : <<publication asset prepare one>>=
const input = assetInput(asset);
let companion = inputs.get(input);
if (!companion) {
    await io.requireFile(input);
    const content = await io.read(input);
    companion = add(content, assetFormat(input, content));
    inputs.set(input, companion);
}
plan.paths.set(assetKey(asset), companion);
```

Collection uses the same bridge and validation as rendering, not a second token
scanner. Unsupported late content prevents all callbacks, so callers can validate
before doing any asset I/O. Contribution rendering here is deliberately inert.

```ts : <<publication asset collection>>=
export function collectAssets(project: ParsedProject, external?: PublicationLinkResolver): {
    assets: PublicationAsset[]; diagnostics: ReturnType<typeof preparePandoc>['diagnostics'];
} {
    const assets: PublicationAsset[] = [];
    const prepared = preparePandoc(project, () => [], asset => { assets.push(asset); return []; }, external);
    return { assets, diagnostics: prepared.diagnostics };
}
```

Generated diagrams and local images share the same content-addressed namespace.
The addition step bounds all retained companion bytes and emits each path once.
SVG is accepted only from the trusted diagram adapter, never as a local image.

Content-derived companion naming is presented in [Portable publication resources](portable_publication_resources.html).

Local files are validated before the optional renderer is invoked. The browser
adapter returns both vector formats, but only the PDF enters the LaTeX body.
Each occurrence retains its location while identical diagram sources render once.

```ts : <<publication diagram companions>>=
const diagrams = assets.filter(asset => asset.kind === 'mermaid');
if (diagrams.length) {
    if (!render) { throw new Error('Mermaid publication requires an explicit browser renderer'); }
    const rendered = await render(diagrams);
    for (const asset of diagrams) {
        const bytes = rendered.get(asset.source);
        if (!bytes || bytes.pdf.length > 16 * 1024 * 1024 || bytes.svg.length > 4 * 1024 * 1024
            || !Buffer.from(bytes.svg).toString('utf8').startsWith('<svg')) {
            throw new Error(`${asset.at.path}:${asset.at.line + 1}: Missing or invalid Mermaid artifacts`);
        }
        assetFormat('diagram.pdf', bytes.pdf);
        plan.paths.set(assetKey(asset), add(bytes.pdf, 'pdf'));
        add(bytes.svg, 'svg');
    }
}
```

## Layout and portability contract

LaTeX measures the escaped caption at the current `\linewidth`, then subtracts
its actual height, depth and spacing from `\textheight`. A graphic fits both the
remaining height and the current width with its aspect ratio preserved. The
non-floating box moves intact to the next page when necessary, including in lists
and quotes. This uses geometry's actual body, never the browser paper size: A5
with top/right/bottom/left 18/16/22/20 mm has a 112 × 170 mm body. Captions consuming
all but 24 pt of the page fail compilation explicitly. Very dense figures can be
unreadable when reduced; no minimum-readable-scale policy is claimed. PDF CropBox
content outside the author-supplied box is not recoverable by layout.

The output `.tex`, fonts and assets share one recoverable binary transaction.
Relocation requires moving the entire output directory. No shell escape is used.
Old orphan companions are not deleted automatically. Concurrent external writers
remain unsupported, as documented by the transaction adapter.
